;; The lockstep (doc/asm/ARM64-SURVEY.md §8, AR5): the program run natively by this arm64 CPU under ptrace (the frame's
;; tracer, emu.Tracee: registers by PTRACE_GETREGSET NT_PRSTATUS, ASLR off) and by a64i from the native's own initial state
;; (its stack's bytes, the auxv's AT_SYSINFO_EHDR made AT_IGNORE in both: no vdso), compared in the sys mode at each system
;; call (the registers, the call's arguments, the bytes write(2) writes, the writable memory) or in the step mode after
;; every instruction (PTRACE_SINGLESTEP: the registers, NZCV, V0-V31, FPSR and FPCR (ARS44), the bytes stored, TPIDR_EL0 after
;; an MSR). The world's
;; answers (read's bytes, clock_gettime's, getrandom's, the ids; in the step mode cntvct_el0's and cntvctss_el0's, AR19) are the
;; native's, copied into a64i. Rules for
;; single-stepping's artifacts: an exclusive region is not stepped (a single-stepped store-exclusive always fails: the step's
;; exception clears the monitor): a64i runs it to its store-exclusive or clrex, the native to a brk at a64i's next pc, compared
;; there (ARS44); a region that does not end so ends the step mode until the next system call, compared there as in the sys mode;
;; x7 at a system call stop is ptrace's (entry 0, exit 1), never compared.
;; AR20 (queue 291): a signal a64i delivered to a handler is the native's signal-delivery stop at the same pc, the native then
;; resumed with the signal (in the step mode stepped with it: its next stop is the handler's first instruction), the frame
;; compared with the rest of memory; the ESR record's fault status of a translation or permission fault is the world's (the
;; kernel makes its tables lazily: the level where a walk stops, a page untouched or read before), taken from the native.

